maya_support: FYI escalation from ACME. They hit repeated 422s when invoking tool calls via our OpenAI-compat endpoint. Ticket C-8421. Repro included below.

sarah_csm: Customer says their feature is blocked, asking for ETA.

jordan_se: Please paste the redacted request/response so we can triage.

maya_support: Pasted (keys redacted):

```json
{
  "model": "openai-compat/v1",
  "messages": [{"role":"user","content":"fetch user 1234"}],
  "tool_call": {
    "name": "get_user",
    "arguments": "{\"userId\": 1234}",
    "status_code": "200"
  }
}
```

Our API returned 422 with this body: 

```json
{
  "error": {
    "message": "tool_call.arguments must be an object, got string",
    "code": 422,
    "details": [{"path": "tool_call.arguments", "expected": "object", "got": "string"}]
  }
}
```

maya_support: Customer notes that many OpenAI client libraries send arguments as a JSON string and sometimes return HTTP fields as strings. They also send responses wrapped like {"payload": {...}}. They expect us to be lenient and normalize.

anatoly_ml: This is a known interoperability gap. Our strict schema validation rejects stringified args and enforces numeric types for status_code. We also don't currently handle single-level envelopes like payload/result/data.

jordan_se: Short-term plan: add a compatibility normalizer in the request pipeline that:
- parses stringified JSON in tool_call.arguments when it's a string;
- coerces numeric-looking strings on common fields (status_code, http_code, code) to integers;
- unwraps one-level envelopes named payload|result|data if present and contains the actual object.

I'll propose a conservative JS transform (pseudo):

```js
function compatNormalize(tc) {
  // parse string args if possible
  if (typeof tc.arguments === 'string') {
    try {
      tc.arguments = JSON.parse(tc.arguments);
    } catch (err) {
      // leave as string, log for debugging
    }
  }
  // coerce numeric-string fields
  ['status_code', 'http_code', 'code'].forEach(k => {
    if (tc[k] && typeof tc[k] === 'string' && /^\d+$/.test(tc[k])) {
      tc[k] = Number(tc[k]);
    }
  });
  // unwrap simple envelopes in result/response objects
  const unwrapKeys = ['payload', 'result', 'data'];
  if (tc.result && typeof tc.result === 'object') {
    for (const k of unwrapKeys) {
      if (tc.result[k] && Object.keys(tc.result).length === 1) {
        tc.result = tc.result[k];
        break;
      }
    }
  }
  // normalize common enum casing
  if (tc.status && typeof tc.status === 'string') {
    const map = { 'ok': 'OK', 'success': 'OK', 'failed': 'FAILED' };
    const lower = tc.status.toLowerCase();
    if (map[lower]) tc.status = map[lower];
  }
  return tc;
}
```

anatoly_ml: We'll add small enum normalization table and log ambiguous values to Sentry for follow-up. Also add unit tests for stringified args, numeric strings, and payload/data/result envelopes.

jordan_se: Deployed a canary build with compat normalizer to edge (canary id c-compat-001). deploy-bot: queued and applied.

deploy-bot: canary deployment complete (c-compat-001) -> infra/edge (sha: df7a9b2).

jordan_se: Early canary logs: ~12% of tool_call payloads had arguments as strings and ~9% had numeric-strings for status_code; parser successfully converted most without error.

maya_support: ACME tested the canary endpoint and reported successful tool invocation — their feature recovered. They did hit one edge case: a response envelope {"data": {"meta": ..., "user": {...}}} where our simple unwrap would remove the outer wrapper but leave nested metadata keys intact which they expect. We agreed to only unwrap single-key envelopes where the wrapper key maps directly to the payload.

jordan_se: Follow-ups:
- add regression tests covering the ACME repro and a few other client variants;
- gate change behind feature flag compat.tool_call_normalization (enabled by default for hosted);
- inform Dedicated/Private teams about opt-in behavior and rollout plan.

